You would think so, but no.  I tried it, with the thinking that not only 
does it give one more point of AC, but you can enchant it higher too.

I thought it would work because it's not metal, but it's not being metal 
that matters for shields.  I didn't find an explanation in the armor 
spoiler, so I looked to the code to explain why the elven shield was 
hindering my spells.  It's the weight.  Then later I found it in the 
spellcasting spoiler:  "Metallic body armor, metallic helmets (except helm 
of brilliance), metallic gloves, metallic shoes, and any shield (although a 
small shield is not as bad) will penalize you."

I seem to recall the small shield made a small difference for some of the 
spells but it was so small I decided to carry the shield anyway.  I think 
all my 0% failure spells were still 0% failure and the others were not much 
higher than with no shield.

